{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793512818,"nonce":"ZZJGn894VFKCcV/lOx2fTmZjDuE=","title":"Compile-heavy runs spend a tenth of their instructions in macOS malloc; mimalloc measured","message":"The first-call optimisation of a static-hot chunk allocates and frees per pass per round (temporary maps and vectors in every pass). In a sample of 200 repeats of method_call's chunk pipeline (Mac arm64, release), _xzm_free and the xzone malloc paths take about 10% and hash-map inserts and rehashes about 17% inclusive.\n\nmimalloc 0.1.52 as zylua's global allocator (default features, no override of C malloc), measured locally and not committed:\n\nMac arm64, instructions retired, warm-up off:\n- method_call chunk pipeline per repeat: 208M -\u003e 167M (-20%); nbody's hot functions -15%\n- whole runs: method_call 677M -\u003e 599M (-11.5%), fib 273M -\u003e 264M (-3%)\n- official files with the official prelude: -7% to -17% (constructs 105.9G -\u003e 87.7G, 11.2 s -\u003e 10.7 s)\n- peak memory footprint: lower on most files (strings 41 -\u003e 28 MB, calls 150 -\u003e 139, nextvar 110 -\u003e 103), higher on api.lua (127 -\u003e 166 MB) and method_call (19.3 -\u003e 22.2 MB). On api.lua peak RSS is lower with mimalloc (217 -\u003e 168 MB): macOS's malloc marks freed pages reusable, which footprint does not count. MIMALLOC_PURGE_DELAY=0 and MIMALLOC_ARENA_EAGER_COMMIT=0 do not change the footprint there.\n\nNUC x86_64 (glibc), perf stat, 7 runs, warm-up off:\n- instructions: fib -2%, method_call -3 to -4%, records -3% (tuned), nbody within noise\n- peak RSS with defaults: fib 46 -\u003e 66 MB, method_call 61 -\u003e 113 MB, nbody 71 -\u003e 116 MB; with MIMALLOC_ARENA_EAGER_COMMIT=0 MIMALLOC_PURGE_DELAY=0: fib 46 -\u003e 46 MB, method_call 61 -\u003e 63 MB\n\nSo the gain is mostly macOS's allocator, and on Linux the default arena commit costs tens of MB. Options: a macOS-only global allocator; options set before the first allocation on Linux; or cutting the churn in the passes themselves (per-pass maps rebuilt every round, `successors` returning a fresh Vec per call, rebuild_cfg_edges rebuilding maps in several passes), which helps both platforms without a memory trade.","files":null}]}